{"name":"Natchez","hex":"#ba9f95","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Natchez","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a tribe of Native Americans once living in the area of Mississippi."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The extinct Muskhogean language of these people.","A city, the county seat of Adams County, Mississippi, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Natchez"}],"entries":[{"title":"Natchez people","blocks":[{"text":"The Natchez were a Native American people who originally lived in the Natchez Bluffs area in the Lower Mississippi Valley, near the present-day city of Natchez, Mississippi, in the United States. The DeSoto chronicle failed to record their presence when they came down the river in 1543. They spoke a language with no known relatives, although it may be distantly related to the Muskogean languages of the Creek Confederacy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Natchez_map.png/330px-Natchez_map.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natchez_people","wikidata":"Q56389"},{"title":"Natchez language","blocks":[{"text":"The Natchez language is the ancestral language of the Natchez people who historically inhabited Mississippi and Louisiana, and who now mostly live among the Muscogee and Cherokee peoples in Oklahoma. The language is considered to be either unrelated to other indigenous languages of the Americas or distantly related to the Muskogean languages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Natchez_lang.png/330px-Natchez_lang.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natchez_language","wikidata":"Q3111838"},{"title":"Natchez (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Natchez is a 2025 American documentary film, directed and produced by Suzannah Herbert. It explores Natchez, Mississippi, which relies on antebellum tourism to survive, reckoning with the past, an uncertain future, and what it owes to descendants of slavery."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c1/Natchez_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natchez_(film)","wikidata":"Q134979718"},{"title":"Natchez (boat)","blocks":[{"text":"Natchez has been the name of several steamboats, and four naval vessels, each named after the city of Natchez, Mississippi or the Natchez people. The current one has been in operation since 1975. The previous Natchez were all operated in the nineteenth century, most by Captain Thomas P. Leathers. Each of the steamboats since Leathers' first had as its ensign a cotton bale between its stacks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/NatchezFlagCCCNov06.jpg/330px-NatchezFlagCCCNov06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natchez_(boat)","wikidata":"Q12062367"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMound_Builder_City.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Chromesun_Photo_emerald01.jpg/500px-Chromesun_Photo_emerald01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Chromesun_Photo_emerald01.jpg/500px-Chromesun_Photo_emerald01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emerald Mound","link":"https://en.